# NOETHERVIM: GIT

Git in NoetherVim comes in two layers. The signs in the margin and the
hunk keys are **core** and work on a fresh install. A full git interface,
the log graph and the diff viewer come from the `tools/git` bundle.

This lesson covers both and says which is which.

Table of contents:

- [The margin](*signs*)
- [Working with hunks](*hunks*)
- [Searching git](*pickers*)
- [The git bundle](*bundle*)
- [Resolving conflicts](*conflicts*)
- [Recap](*recap*)

## THE MARGIN *signs*

In a file inside a git repository, the left margin marks every line you have
changed since the last commit: added, changed or removed. The statusline
carries the branch name and a count of the three.

A run of adjacent changed lines is a **hunk**, which is the unit git thinks
in and the unit these keys act on.

Moving between them is directional, so it is the bracket pair:

    `]h` `[h`   next and previous hunk

## WORKING WITH HUNKS *hunks*

All of these are core, under `<Leader>h`.

    `<Leader>hp`   preview this hunk, without leaving the buffer
    `<Leader>hs`   stage this hunk
    `<Leader>hr`   reset this hunk, discarding the change
    `<Leader>hu`   undo the last stage
    `<Leader>hS`   stage the whole buffer
    `<Leader>hR`   reset the whole buffer
    `<Leader>hb`   blame this line: who changed it, when, and in what commit
    `<Leader>hd`   diff this file against the index

In visual mode, `<Leader>hs` and `<Leader>hr` act on the selected lines
rather than the whole hunk, which is how you stage part of a change.

`<Leader>hp` is the one to build a habit around. Reviewing a hunk before
staging it catches the debug line you left in, and it costs one key.

Fill in the key that moves to the next changed hunk:

Next hunk in this buffer: ___

## SEARCHING GIT *pickers*

Under SearchLeader, because none of these change anything:

    `<Space>Gs`   status: every changed file
    `<Space>Gc`   the commit log
    `<Space>Gb`   commits touching this buffer
    `<Space>Gf`   files tracked by git
    `<Space>GS`   stashes
    `<Space>fG`   find a file among the changed ones

`<Space>Gb` is the fastest way to answer "when did this file last change
and why". `<Space>Gc` with a search term is the fastest way to find the
commit you half remember.

## THE GIT BUNDLE *bundle*

Everything above is core. `tools/git` adds the interfaces:

    `<C-w>F`    a full TUI git client: stage, commit, push, branch
    `<C-w>[d`   open the diff viewer
    `<C-w>]d`   close it
    `:Git`      any git command, with the output in a buffer
    `:Flog`     the log graph

The diff viewer is the one worth the bundle on its own. It shows every
changed file in a project side by side, with the file list down one edge,
and it does the same for any commit or range in the history.

`:Git` is fugitive. `:Git blame` opens an interactive blame beside the
file; `:Git log`, `:Git commit` and the rest behave as you would expect
with the output in a real buffer you can search and yank from.

Enable it with `<Space>cb`, then restart. It needs `libgit2` on your system
for the TUI client; `:checkhealth noethervim` says so if it is missing.

## RESOLVING CONFLICTS *conflicts*

The bundle also handles merge conflicts. When a file has conflict markers,
the two sides are highlighted rather than left as raw `<<<<<<<` text, and
keys are offered to take one side, the other, both, or neither, and to move
between the conflicts in the file.

This is the part that most repays having the bundle enabled before you need
it, since a conflict is a bad moment to be installing tooling.

